    "### 3.2 Discussion on Advantages and Disadvantages of Tests"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The Chi-Square test, Kolmogorov-Smirnov (KS) test, and Shapiro-Wilk test yield generally favorable results, validating the success of the proposed modeling framework. However, some exceptions emerge in specific tests and columns, which stem from the fundamental mechanisms and limitations of each test. This warrants a systematic discussion of their respective advantages and disadvantages.\n",
    "#### Chi-Square Test\n",
    "\n",
    "The Chi-Square test operates by comparing the observed data frequencies against the expected frequencies derived from the hypothesized distribution. Its primary advantage lies in its versatility: it is applicable to categorical data and can handle discrete distributions with clear binning structures. Additionally, its underlying principle—assessing the \"goodness-of-fit\" through frequency discrepancies—is intuitive and easy to interpret, making it accessible for researchers across disciplines.\n",
    "\n",
    "Yet, these strengths are accompanied by critical limitations. First, the Chi-Square test is highly sensitive to sample size: it demands a sufficiently large number of observations (often requiring expected frequencies ≥5 in most bins) to ensure the asymptotic approximation of the Chi-Square distribution holds. This explains its failure in ( X_1 ), where the initial random distributions exhibit substantial variation and the sample size may be inadequate. Second, the test’s reliance on frequency comparison makes it less efficient when evaluating purely random distributions, as the inherent stochasticity can amplify discrepancies between observed and expected counts even when the underlying model is correct.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Kolmogorov-Smirnov Test\n",
    "In contrast, the KS test addresses some of the Chi-Square test’s shortcomings. It does not require binning and directly compares the empirical cumulative distribution function (ECDF) of the data with the theoretical distribution function. This non-parametric nature renders it more robust to variations in sample size, as its performance is less dependent on large datasets compared to the Chi-Square test. This is reflected in our results, where the KS test provides consistent validation across all ( X_i ) without the sample-size-related failures observed in the Chi-Square test.\n",
    "\n",
    "However, the KS test has its own limitations. Most notably, it is insensitive to tail behavior, meaning it may fail to detect significant discrepancies between distributions in the extreme tails. Fortunately, in our case, the ( X_i ) distributions of the two datasets do not exhibit substantial tail differences, so this limitation does not compromise our conclusions. Additionally, the KS test is most powerful for continuous distributions and may lose efficiency for discrete or heavily discretized data.\n",
    "#### Shapiro-Wilk Test\n",
    "\n",
    "The Shapiro-Wilk test is specifically designed to assess whether a dataset follows a normal distribution. Its primary advantage is its high power for detecting deviations from normality, especially for small to moderate sample sizes (between 3 and 5000 observations). This makes it an ideal choice when testing the assumption of normality, which is required for many statistical techniques.\n",
    "\n",
    "However, the Shapiro-Wilk test has several limitations. First, it is only applicable to testing normality and cannot be used for other distribution types. Second, it has strict sample size limitations (valid only for n between 3 and 5000). Third, like many significance tests, it can be overly sensitive with large sample sizes, rejecting the null hypothesis even for trivial deviations from normality that may not be practically significant. Fourth, it is sensitive to outliers, which can lead to false conclusions about the normality of the underlying distribution.\n",

    "#### Summary and Practical Implications\n",
    "In conclusion, the choice between these tests depends on the specific characteristics of the data and the research objectives:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Chi-Square test** is preferred for categorical data or when interpretability of frequency-based comparisons is critical, but it requires large sample sizes to avoid spurious results.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **KS test** is more suitable for continuous distributions and situations with limited samples, though researchers must exercise caution when tail behavior is of interest.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Shapiro-Wilk test** is the test of choice when specifically assessing normality, particularly for small to moderate sample sizes, but it is limited to this single distribution type.\n",
    "\n",
    "Our analysis demonstrates that combining these tests can provide a more comprehensive validation: the Chi-Square test offers intuitive frequency-based insights, the KS test compensates with robustness to smaller samples, and the Shapiro-Wilk test provides targeted assessment of normality assumptions. Their complementary strengths reinforce the reliability of our modeling results, despite the individual limitations observed.""
   ]
  },
